2026-09-16T11:30Z claude note Reimagined and confirmed by reproduction: both strays have regrown (/tmp/.git and /scratch/tmp/.git, still empty), so the diagnosis was re-verified live rather than taken on faith. Isolated a minimal repro -- a throwaway module built under $TMPDIR importing net/http -- and `go list -deps .` there prints exactly `error obtaining VCS status: exit status 128 / Use -buildvcs=false to disable VCS stamping.`, the tool naming its own fix. Adding `-buildvcs=false` to the single `go list -deps .` call at tests/test_contract.sh:98 makes the negative control detect net/http again under the same stray-.git conditions; the flag only suppresses VCS stamping; it does not touch import resolution, so the control keeps its teeth (also empirically distinct from the DEPS_OK `go list` at line 41, which runs from the repo's own module root and finds this repo's real .git before ever walking above $TMPDIR, so it was never at risk and needs no change). This is the minimal repo-level fix and it is the only one Action 1 asks for. Action 2 (should `make` continue past a failing suite) is scope beyond this ticket's own exit criteria, which name only the buildvcs symptom -- track it separately if pursued, don't fold it into this fix.
2026-09-16T16:25Z claude note Blind-Spot pass, one correction and one confirmation. Correction: the 11:15Z claim that the verify/reproducibility failure "shared this cause" is inferred from correlation (one rmdir cleared both) and does NOT reproduce independently -- make verify was run standalone under the live stray-.git conditions and PASSED, because its own mktemp workdir receives a git clone that creates a valid .git inside it, so Go's upward walk stops there and never reaches /tmp. Read that line as "not reproduced independently", not as settled, and diagnose any future verify FAIL fresh rather than as a recurrence of this ticket. Confirmation: tests/*.sh was re-grepped for every mktemp-plus-go-build/list/vet combination, and test_contract.sh:98 is the only call site that builds inside a bare mktemp -d -- every other go invocation runs from src/go, which has this repo's real .git adjacent. So the one-line fix is sufficient to carry make check end to end.
2026-09-16T17:05Z claude note Plan pass: the red step is the existing offline negative control at tests/test_contract.sh:98, but as written it only turns red when the *host* happens to have a stray empty .git above $TMPDIR -- exactly the non-determinism this ticket exists to kill, so CI (no stray .git, pinned Go 1.21) would never notice a regression here. Recommend a self-contained assertion in the same block: create an empty .git directly at $WORK (one level above $NEG, the mktemp root the negative control already uses) before invoking `go list -deps .` in $NEG, manufacturing the adversarial condition inside the test rather than borrowing it from the host. RED today: without -buildvcs=false that call errors on the manufactured stray .git and finds no net/http, reproducing the defect deterministically on any host. GREEN: after adding -buildvcs=false to the line-98 call, the same manufactured-ancestor run still finds net/http, and the pre-existing ambient-state check (lines 96-99, unmodified environment) keeps passing -- a stray-ancestor arm plus a clean-host arm. Antipattern verdict: the existing line-98 assertion is not itself tautological (it invokes the real go toolchain, not a restatement of the code under test), only incomplete as a regression guard; the fix is to add the manufactured fixture, not replace anything. Beyond that one addition, no new committed test is warranted -- Action 1 is exactly the one-line flag change, and the existing suite plus this fixture is sufficient.
2026-09-16T10:33Z claude note Executed. Two arms, not one: arm 1 keeps the pre-existing ambient-environment assertion, arm 2 manufactures an empty .git at $WORK/neg-offline-stray one level above its own throwaway module, so the adversarial condition is built by the test instead of borrowed from the host. Deviation from the Plan pass, deliberate: the plan said mkdir $WORK/.git, but $NEG is a direct child of $WORK, so that would have poisoned the clean-host arm too and collapsed the two arms into one. A sibling subtree gives both arms honestly. Red step watched fail before the flag was added. Four-way control matrix on /dev/shm (the one filesystem on this host with no stray ancestor): clean+no-flag FOUND, stray+no-flag NOT-FOUND, stray+flag FOUND, clean+flag FOUND -- so the manufactured stray alone causes the red, and the flag alone clears it. Whole suite under TMPDIR=/dev/shm confirms the point the ticket cared about: on a stray-free host arm 1 passes with or without the fix (no teeth there, which is why CI never saw this), while arm 2 fails without it and passes with it. make check now exits 0 end to end, 0 FAIL across 37 suites; the host strays /tmp/.git and /scratch/tmp/.git are both present and regrown, and no longer matter. Action 2 deliberately not folded in -- it is ticket 0290.
2026-09-16T11:05Z claude note Round 3 closed the third and last door. Verified against a deliberately reverted copy of the fix, eight cases: arm 2 PASSes under the fix in a plain environment, under an exported GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false, and under the go env config file (exercised through a throwaway GOENV path, so the author's own go env was never written -- confirmed empty before and after); it FAILs in all three of those under the reverted copy; and it SKIPs, never passes, with git absent from PATH. The review's round-2 dissent is recorded and unresolved, an author call: correctness held that a machine carrying an ambient GOFLAGS cannot suffer the underlying defect either, so skipping there was defensible. Pinning the environment makes the question moot -- the arm now measures the code rather than the machine. CI was never at risk in any of the three variants.

--- body ---
## Context

`make check` aborts at `Makefile:56`:

```
FAIL: offline (neg control): detector failed to flag net/http
contract: 16 passed, 1 failed, 0 warned, 1 skipped
```

**The cause is not the Go version.** An earlier revision of this ticket said so
and was wrong. Measured on this host:

- `/tmp/.git` and `/scratch/tmp/.git` both exist, and both are **empty
  directories** — not repositories, just directories with that name.
- `go list -deps ./...` run from the module root (`src/go/`) exits 0 on this
  same Go 1.22.2, with and without `-buildvcs=false`. The toolchain is fine.

The chain is:

1. The offline negative control in `tests/test_contract.sh` builds its probe in
   a `mktemp` working directory under `$TMPDIR`. (It was line 98 on
   `origin/main` before this ticket; the fix moved it.)
2. Go's buildvcs walks *upward* from that directory looking for a VCS root and
   finds `/tmp/.git`.
3. It runs `git` there; the directory is empty, so git exits 128.
4. `go list` returns empty output.
5. The probe's grep finds no `net/http`, and the **negative control** reports
   that the detector failed to flag it.

So the Go version matters only in that it determines whether that upward walk
happens at all. The defect is the interaction between a stray `.git` above the
temp root and a probe that builds there.

## Why it clears the severity floor

**It is the negative control that fails.** The failing assertion is the one
claiming the detector *can see* a violation. When it fails, the adjacent
`offline` PASS reports only that the detector said nothing — which is what a
blind detector does. The failure voids the result beside it.

**It halts the cascade.** `make` stops at the failing target, so every suite
ordered after `test-contract` never runs. A contributor reporting "make check
passed" has run a strict subset without being told which. That happened
repeatedly during `/raid 255 256 278 279`, including in reports I wrote.

**It was re-diagnosed from scratch at least four times** in a single session,
by three reviewers and by me, each paying the diagnosis cost again. The cost is
not the failure; it is the rediscovery.

## Two fixes, and they are not alternatives

**Host-level**: `rmdir /tmp/.git /scratch/tmp/.git`. **Done 2026-09-16** —
both were empty, and `make check` went green end to end immediately after. This
fixes every session on this machine, but a host can regrow a stray at any time
and adopters have their own hosts, so it settles nothing durable.

**Repo-level**: make the probe immune. Either pass `-buildvcs=false` where it
invokes `go list`, or point the probe's `TMPDIR` at a directory it controls.
This is the durable half — the host fix is a cleanup, not a guarantee.

Do the repo-level one. The host-level one is worth doing anyway, and is not
this ticket's deliverable.

## The second failure behind it

With the contract probe cleared, the run reaches the next target and fails:

```
verify: FAIL -- committed binary is NOT reproducible
```

**Confirmed same cause, 2026-09-16.** Both stray directories were removed
(`rmdir`, both empty) and `make check` then ran end to end on the same
toolchain: `ALL TESTS PASSED`, `CHECK PASSED (test + validate)`. So the
reproducibility failure was the same buildvcs artifact, not a genuine
difference in the committed binary. One cause, two symptoms, and the second was
only ever reachable once the first was cleared — which is what a halting
cascade hides.

## What is left after the host cleanup

The symptom is gone here; the ticket is not. The probe is still unable to tell
"no network import" from "I could not look", and the next stray `.git` on any
host reproduces it exactly. The remaining deliverable is Action 1: make the
probe immune, so its verdict means what it claims regardless of what sits above
its working directory.

## Relevant files

- `tests/test_contract.sh` — the offline probe and its negative control. Named
  by construct, not by line: the arms and their `neg_offline_*` helpers moved
  when this ticket was executed, and a pinned line number would have rotted on
  the very commit that fixed the defect.
- `Makefile:56` — the target where the cascade stops
- `Makefile:120` — the `verify` target that fails next
- `.github/workflows/CI.yml` — pins Go 1.21, which is why CI never sees any of it

## Actions

1. Make the probe immune to a stray `.git` above its working directory
   (`-buildvcs=false`, or a controlled `TMPDIR`). Confirm the fix does not
   weaken what the probe detects: the flag suppresses VCS stamping, not
   dependency resolution, but this is a negative control, and a weakened one is
   worse than a red one.
2. Consider whether `make check` should continue past a failing suite, or at
   minimum name the suites it did not reach. A cascade that stops silently
   turns one local red into an unreliable green for everything behind it. This
   run is the evidence: the reproducibility failure existed the whole time and
   was simply unreachable behind the halt.

   **Split out to ticket 0290 and deliberately outside this ticket's exit
   criteria.** Action 1 makes one probe honest; Action 2 changes how `make`
   reports every suite. Different blast radius, different sign-off unit.

## Test

**Red step (self-contained, not host-borrowed):** in `tests/test_contract.sh`'s
offline negative control (~line 90-99), before the `go list -deps .` call at
line 98, `mkdir -p "$WORK/.git"` — an empty directory one level above `$NEG`,
the mktemp root the negative control already builds its throwaway module
under. This manufactures the exact adversarial condition inside the test
instead of relying on whatever happens to sit above the host's own `$TMPDIR`,
which is what made the original failure host-dependent and invisible to CI
(pinned Go 1.21, no stray `.git`) in the first place.

- **RED today:** `(cd "$NEG" && go list -deps .)` errors on the manufactured
  stray `.git` and reports no `net/http`, so the negative control fails —
  reproducing the defect deterministically, on any host, with or without an
  ambient stray directory.
- **GREEN after the fix:** adding `-buildvcs=false` to that same line-98 call
  makes it find `net/http` even with `$WORK/.git` present. The pre-existing
  assertion at lines 96-99 (unmodified environment, no manufactured ancestor)
  must keep passing too — both the stray-ancestor arm and the clean-host arm
  green confirms the flag suppresses VCS stamping only, not import resolution.

No other new committed test is warranted: Action 1 is the one-line flag
change, and the existing suite plus this one manufactured fixture is
sufficient to guard it without inventing a parallel mechanism.

**As built** — the plan above is kept as written, since it is the record of
what was decided; two things came out differently and this is where they are
reconciled. The line numbers quoted above (`98`, `96-99`) are those of
`origin/main` before the fix and no longer resolve.

1. The stray went in a **sibling subtree** (`$WORK/neg-offline-stray/.git`),
   not at `$WORK`. `$NEG` is a direct child of `$WORK`, so a stray there would
   have poisoned the ambient arm too and collapsed the intended two arms into
   one.
2. Arm 2 **proves its own red condition before crediting its green**, and runs
   **with the environment pinned**. Three review rounds found the same defect
   class through three doors, each time a way for arm 2 to pass without having
   tested anything: `git` absent from `PATH` (buildvcs skips stamping silently,
   so nothing was ever blinded); an exported `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false`; and
   `go env -w`, which an empty-but-set `GOFLAGS` falls through to. The last two
   are exactly what someone hitting this bug would set as a workaround.

   Closed by running the unflagged probe first and reporting SKIP when the
   manufactured stray fails to blind it, and by shutting both ambient doors
   (`GOFLAGS=`, `GOENV=off`) so the explicit argument is the only flag in play.
   Verified against a deliberately reverted copy of the fix: arm 2 now fails in
   all three environments where `git` is present, and skips rather than passes
   where it is not.

   Worth recording that the guard for this defect kept reproducing the defect:
   an all-clear indistinguishable from "I could not look" is what the ticket is
   about, and it took three rounds to stop writing one.

## Verification

- [x] The probe passes with an empty `.git` sitting above its temp root
      (arm 2, `$WORK/neg-offline-stray/.git`, manufactured inside the test)
- [x] The probe still flags a real `net/http` import (both arms assert exactly
      that; the flag suppresses stamping only)
- [x] `make check` runs end to end — exit 0, 0 FAIL across all 37 suites.
      Read this as "nothing halts the cascade any more", which is what this
      ticket can deliver. Making `make` *report* the suites it never reached
      when something does fail is 0290's job, not this box's.
- [ ] Still green on Go 1.21, matching CI — no 1.21 toolchain on this host and
      fetching one would breach the offline contract; CI is the check.
      `-buildvcs` has been a shared build flag (build/install/list/test) since
      Go 1.18, so acceptance is not in doubt -- and a toolchain that did
      reject it would make both arms go red, not pass, since
      `neg_offline_detects` swallows stderr. A silent regression is the
      one failure mode this cannot have.

## Invariants

- The offline probe must keep detecting a real `net/http` import. A fix that
  makes the control pass by weakening what it inspects is a regression wearing
  a green check.

## Exit criteria

- A stray `.git` above the temp root can no longer make `make check` report a
  failure that has nothing to do with the code under test

## Related

- 0285 — also surfaced by review during the same raid, also pre-existing, also
  invisible from this repo's own happy path.
